<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dromahair</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dromahair is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-leitrim">County Leitrim</a>, Ireland, on the River Bonet near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-gill">Lough Gill</a>. It was the main seat of the O'Rourke lords of Breifne and later West Breifne, and in 1152 it was the scene of the abduction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbforgaill">Derbforgaill</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigernan-ua-ruairc">Tigernán Ua Ruairc</a>, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, an episode later linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-norman-invasion-of-ireland">Norman invasion of Ireland</a>. The ruins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/creevelea-friary">Creevelea Friary</a> lie nearby.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dromahair.jpg" alt="Dromahair" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Oliver Dixon · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_Street,_Dromahair_-_geograph.org.uk_-_799301.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dromahair, in Irish Droim Dhá Thiar, apparently meaning the ridge of two demons, takes its name from the ridge of high ground on which it stands above the River Bonet, which flows into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-gill">Lough Gill</a>. It lies in the hilly north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-leitrim">County Leitrim</a>, 10 kilometres from Manorhamilton and 17 kilometres from Sligo. Much of the village was laid out by the Earl of Leitrim on the model of a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, and its central streets still follow his plan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The ridge was the site of Drumlease, an important early church site and a Patrician foundation of the fifth century. During the medieval period Dromahair was the capital of Breifne, a Gaelic confederation stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kells">Kells</a> across County Cavan and north Leitrim to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-sligo">County Sligo</a>, and the seat of the O'Rourke kings, whose castle, built around 950, survives as ruins with its banqueting hall. From here Devorgilla, wife of Tiernan O'Rourke, was carried off to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferns">Ferns</a> by Dermot McMurrough, King of Leinster, starting a feud that led to McMurrough's exile from Ireland.</p><p>Creevelea Abbey, a Franciscan friary founded in 1508 as a daughter house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donegal-friary">Donegal Abbey</a>, was in use until the 17th century, when the New Model Army forced the friars out. A fortified house was built for Sir William Villiers around 1629. In 1798 the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-joseph-amable-humbert">General Humbert</a>, pursued through Leitrim after its victories at Castlebar and Carricknagat, rested at Dromahair and threw captured British artillery into the Bonet. William Butler Yeats named the village in his poem The Man Who Dreamed of Faeryland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The nave, choir, tower and transept of Creevelea Abbey are well preserved, and the friary is protected as a national monument. The railway station, opened on 1 September 1881 on the Sligo, Leitrim and Northern Counties Railway, closed with the line on 1 October 1957. Parke's Castle stands on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lough-gill">Lough Gill</a> to the north-west.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dromahair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Dromahair</b>, by Lough Gill, became the O'Rourke seat.</blockquote><a href="/state/west-breifne">West Breifne</a></li></ul></section>
